## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior

Found by lane-enum while generalising B089 (2026-08-31). Pinned, not fixed:
`a_workspace_free_function_overload_set_is_a_different_gap` holds the case so
nobody reads that silence as something B089 caused.

## Defect

When a workspace FREE FUNCTION has two or more overloads, a call to it reaches
**no argument checking of any kind** — not arity, not primitive types, not
funcdef slots.

`lookup_function_return` returns `None` the moment two workspace symbols share
a name (the deliberate "ambiguous, say nothing" arm — the same arm B084 fixed
for *crediting*). `call_type` then falls through to the `has_global_ident` arm,
which suppresses the undefined-identifier diagnostic and only walks the
arguments for their own errors. Nothing compares them to any signature.

Workspace METHODS do not have this hole: they produce real overload sets via
`lookup_method_overloads_with_inheritance`.

## How it was found, and why that matters

**It was found by a test passing when it should not have.** lane-enum's first
draft of B089's over-firing gate asserted that an overload set with one matching
candidate stays silent — and it passed VACUOUSLY, because the set logic never
ran at all on a free-function set. It was rewritten onto methods, which produce
real sets.

So the discovery came from asking "why did my negative case pass?" rather than
from a failing test. Worth stating because it is the same family as B088's
vacuous silence and B097's non-discriminating control: **a test that passes for
a reason you have not identified is not yet evidence.**

## Relationship to B084

B084 fixed the CREDITING half of this same arm: an overloaded global function
was called and never recorded as used. This is the CHECKING half of the same
arm, still open. One `lookup_function_return` restriction, two consequences,
one fixed and one not.

## Work

Give the Ident-callee path an overload-set entry point, the way the method path
already has one. Note this is the direction that ADDS diagnostics, so it needs
the B060 treatment: measure the false-positive surface across the plugin corpora
before believing it, and pin an over-firing gate that a real overload set
actually exercises — which, per the discovery above, means checking that the
gate is not vacuous.

## Test plan

Red: a workspace free function overloaded 2x, called with an argument matching
NO candidate, must diagnose. Generalise per CLAUDE.md across arity mismatch,
primitive type mismatch, and a funcdef-slot mismatch (B089's subject), each in
both the bare and namespaced spelling.

Required silences in the same fixture: a call matching exactly one candidate; a
call matching one of three; and — the gate that catches a vacuous suite — an
assertion that the overload-set path actually EXECUTED for these cases, not just
that no diagnostic appeared.

## Related

B089 (which pinned this and fixed the reachable positions), B084 (the crediting
half of the same arm), B060 (the false-positive precedent for widening an
argument rule), B088 / B097 (the same "why did this pass?" family).